RayCore hears every change to an extension's storage, every firing of its alarms and every change to its
declarativeNetRequest rules, so the WebKit copy of the extension (macOS 15.4+, a replica of Chromium's) sees the same
state. StorageFrontend::OnSettingsChanged calls g_ray_extension_storage_changed with the context, the id, the area
("local", "sync", "managed", "session") and the changes, before it looks for listeners, so a change reaches the app even
when Chromium's copy has none. AlarmManager::OnAlarm calls g_ray_extension_alarm_fired with the context, the id and
the alarm before it hands the alarm to its delegate, so the WebKit copy's onAlarm fires for the same alarm Chromium's
does. RulesMonitorService wraps the callback of UpdateDynamicRules, UpdateSessionRules, UpdateEnabledStaticRulesets and
UpdateStaticRules: once one succeeds it calls g_ray_dnr_rules_changed with the context, the id and what changed
("dynamic", "session", "rulesets"), holding the context by a WeakPtr so a reply after the profile is gone calls nothing. //chrome/browser/ray (ac_extension_storage.cc, ac_extension_alarms.cc,
ac_extension_dnr.cc) sets the hooks the first time the app asks for them and passes on only changes for extensions the
app watches. Unset, Chrome behaves as stock.

